\newlabel{eq:signature}{{1}{3}{Inputs and tree signatures}{equation.2.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Schematic representation of a tree-signature calculation. In panel A, gene trees are compared either with two reference species trees using one dissimilarity, or with one reference species tree using two dissimilarities. Either view alone has limited resolution, whereas their combination distinguishes the illustrated groups. Panel B shows the signature of one gene tree across combinations of reference trees and dissimilarities. The current implementation treats gene trees as unrooted and reference species trees as rooted, although some dissimilarities ignore the reference root. Panel C shows the resulting design matrix used by downstream analyses. }}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{figure01}{{1}{4}{Schematic representation of a tree-signature calculation. In panel A, gene trees are compared either with two reference species trees using one dissimilarity, or with one reference species tree using two dissimilarities. Either view alone has limited resolution, whereas their combination distinguishes the illustrated groups. Panel B shows the signature of one gene tree across combinations of reference trees and dissimilarities. The current implementation treats gene trees as unrooted and reference species trees as rooted, although some dissimilarities ignore the reference root. Panel C shows the resulting design matrix used by downstream analyses}{figure.caption.1}{}}
